   "url": "https://jason-shi-f9dm.squarespace.com/s/PUMaC2010_AlgebraA.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "9-12",
  "points": null,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "numeric",
  "prompt": "Let $p$ be a polynomial with integer coefficients such that $p(15)=6$, $p(22)=1196$, and $p(35)=26$. Find an integer $n$ such that $p(n)=n+82$.",
  "image": null,
  "choices": null,
  "answer": "28",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Use divisibility of values",
    "body": "If $p(n)=n+82$ then $x-n$ divides $p(x)-x-82$ as a polynomial with integer coefficients, so for every integer $a$, $a-n$ divides $p(a)-a-82$.\n\nThus $15-n\\mid p(15)-15-82=-91$ and $35-n\\mid p(35)-35-82=-91$. So $15-n$ and $35-n$ are divisors of $91$ (among $\\pm1,\\pm7,\\pm13,\\pm91$) that differ by $20$. The only possibilities are $(15-n,35-n)=(-13,7)$ giving $n=28$, or $(-7,13)$ giving $n=22$.\n\nBut $n=22$ is impossible because $p(22)=1196\\neq22+82$. So $n=28$.\n\n**Answer: 28**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "polynomials",
   "number-theory"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
